WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.819
It’s a big day!

00:00:01.819 --> 00:00:06.110
You just moved in with your boo and you’re
totally rocking this living-together thing.

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:10.690
But then comes the first rent bill and you’re
stuck wondering...how do we handle this?

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:14.809
If you asked your grandparents how they handled
finances back in their day, you might

00:00:14.809 --> 00:00:16.510
be met with some weird looks.

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:20.180
After all, when they moved in together, they
were most likely already hitched and it was

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:24.500
just assumed that they’d immediately combine
every aspect of their separate lives.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:30.180
The house, the Studebaker, Aunt Mildred’s
antique tea set...everything became family

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:31.180
property.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:32.579
Including the bank accounts.

00:00:32.579 --> 00:00:38.390
In 1960, 65% of children grew up in a household
where the mother was a homemaker and the father

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:39.390
was the breadwinner.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:42.430
Today, only 22% of children grow up that way.

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:47.330
From a dramatic rise in women being the breadwinners
to falling marriage rates the relational and

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:50.000
economic landscape has changed dramatically.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.500
These days, 25% of parents living with a child
are unmarried and 35% of unmarried parents

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:56.500
are cohabitating.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:02.530
So the question is: should you consolidate
finances, and continue a time-honored tradition?

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:05.500
Or is maintaining financial autonomy more
important?

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:18.980
When we asked you on Twitter, 79% of you said
you combine all or some of your finances with

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:19.990
your partner.

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:21.570
And this tracks with national trends.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:23.040
But this is changing.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.860
Millenials that live together are now more
likely to keep separate finances than any

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:29.220
previous generation.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:33.500
And it turns out there are some pretty compelling
advantages to keeping your finances separate

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.060
as a couple.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:39.350
Americans with student loan debt carry an
average of over $32,000.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:43.460
And the typical American is floating over
$6,000 in credit card debts.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:48.329
With numbers like these, it’s easy to understand
why one partner might feel guilty burdening

00:01:48.329 --> 00:01:51.170
the other with something that was “their
problem”.

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:56.299
Or perhaps you’ve been in or witnessed a
financially abusive relationship in the past.

00:01:56.299 --> 00:01:59.700
Separate accounts can help protect you if
serious problems arise.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:03.899
If you’ve ever known somebody that suffered
from a gambling or substance addiction, you

00:02:03.899 --> 00:02:07.090
know first hand the financial devastation
that can follow.

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:12.200
But let’s be real - the main reason people
keep separate finances is to maintain their

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:13.430
independence!

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:17.400
In modern society, it’s completely normal
to enter your 30’s without ever sharing

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.800
your finances with anyone else.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:21.900
As your lives begin to merge, it’s understandable

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:24.330
to feel protective about your autonomy.

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:28.390
You’ve never had somebody telling you what
you can and can’t buy before.

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:29.690
Why start now?

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:33.680
And if you don’t have to talk about it,
you don’t have to fight about it, right?

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:38.290
As attractive as it might be go “Lone Wolf”,
there are some good reasons the majority of

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:40.430
couples still combine finances.

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:44.050
So let’s look at what the “Shared Approach”
has going for it.

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:49.330
When money is shared, you can work efficiently
toward big goals like debt reduction.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:53.350
Buying a home is easier when you have both
of your incomes and credit histories available

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:54.629
to work with.

00:02:54.629 --> 00:02:59.409
And if you plan on spending retirement together,
pooling resources makes a lot of sense.

00:02:59.409 --> 00:03:03.411
Think about it: is one of you gonna spend
your golden years in a nice condo and the

00:03:03.411 --> 00:03:05.180
other in the tool shed?

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:06.650
Probably not.

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:12.000
Shared finances can also protect you against
unexpected pitfalls, like an injury or layoff.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.220
And if you enter a tight season by choice--
such as taking time off to start a family

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:21.690
or navigate a career transition - anyone will
tell you what a game changer it is for somebody

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:23.890
to back you up financially.

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:29.810
Shared cooperation and transparency can also
result in a healthier, longer-lasting relationship.

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:36.290
A 2018 study found that 20% of relationships
that don’t share finances end because of

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:40.720
money problems…compared to only 4% of relationships
that do.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:45.739
And a series of five university studies found
that couples who pool all their money are

00:03:45.739 --> 00:03:49.459
happier in their relationships and less likely
to break up.

00:03:49.459 --> 00:03:53.110
And don’t AT us with the correlation/causation
argument.

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:57.780
The researchers specifically noted that this
wasn’t merely a correlation but that the

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:03.060
“results demonstrate that method of account
management can.. influence relationship quality.”

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:08.030
So it’s pretty clear that how you handle
money actually affects your relationship.

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:10.849
There are also hybrid versions of the two.

00:04:10.849 --> 00:04:15.620
Like the “Proportional Earnings Approach”
where each partner contributes the same percentage

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:18.620
of their income into a joint account.

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:23.570
Or “Bill Parsing”, where each person picks
certain bills and expenses to pay for, then

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:27.170
use Splitwise or Venmo to split large items
like rent.

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:30.260
Back to the big question: which is more
important?

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:32.320
The teamwork or autonomy?

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:36.009
We here at Two Cents believe you can have
BOTH if you work at it!

00:04:36.009 --> 00:04:39.330
For example, we got hitched young, while still
in college.

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:44.449
Truthfully, there wasn’t much to pool, so
we went the traditional path by default.

00:04:44.449 --> 00:04:49.850
But over the years, conflicts arose because
neither of us had much spending independence.

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:53.770
We hear this from couples all the time, and
it can really start to build resentment over

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:54.770
the years.

00:04:54.770 --> 00:05:00.810
So our solution was to “have it both ways”:
we combined our finances, but we created individual,

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:05.759
equally-funded envelopes of “fun-money”
to spend or save however we saw fit.

00:05:05.759 --> 00:05:08.410
So I get guilt-free thrift store runs.

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:12.520
And I don’t need permission to trick out
my mountain bike.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:14.580
Boom!

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:19.750
Each relationship is unique and there is ultimately
no “one size fits all”.

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:25.210
But if you have decided to share a future
together and have healthy levels of trust

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:30.710
we encourage you to use your finances as a
way to work better as partners, while still

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:34.200
protecting individual freedom and independence.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.660
And that’s our Two Cents!

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:40.720
Thanks to our patrons for keeping Two Cents financially healthy.

